<h1>Breece Hall's Quad Injury: <em>Explosive, Week-to-Week</em></h1>
<div class="meta-row">
<span>📖 5 min read</span>
<span>🏈 Football</span>
<span>⚠️ Jets · Week-to-Week</span>
</div>
<p class="dek">Breece Hall left the Jets' Week 3 loss in Detroit with a right quadriceps injury and has been ruled week-to-week. Hall is coming off the first 1,000-yard rushing season by a Jet in a decade and just signed a three-year extension, and his 2022 combine remains one of the most explosive running back workouts on record. Here is the athletic profile behind the back the Jets can least afford to lose.</p>
</div><div class="content">
<div class="toc">
<div class="toc-label">In This Article</div>
<a href="#injury">What Happened</a>
<a href="#combine">The 2022 Combine Profile</a>
<a href="#outlook">The Jets' Backfield Without Him</a>
<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="injury">What Happened</h2>
<p>Hall was tackled near the sideline in the fourth quarter of New York's 31-24 loss to the Lions on September 27 and did not return. He said afterward that an MRI was likely, and the early reporting was encouraging: the scan reportedly brought "good news," though Hall was still ruled week-to-week and was expected to miss the Week 4 game at Chicago. Per that reporting, the Jets could avoid an injured reserve stint if they believe he can return in under four weeks.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🩹</div>
<div class="stat-title">Breece Hall — Injury Snapshot</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Week 3 at Detroit Lions · Right Quadriceps</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">163</div><div class="label">Rushing Yards, Weeks 1–3</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">1,065</div><div class="label">Rushing Yards in 2025</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">$45.75M</div><div class="label">Max Value, 3-Year Extension</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>It has been a bumpy stretch for the 25-year-old. He strained his groin in practice in mid-August and was projected to miss two to three weeks, but he was back for the opener and ran for 102 yards and a touchdown at Tennessee. He managed 29 yards against Green Bay in Week 2 and 32 on 13 carries against Detroit before leaving. Last season he set a career high with 1,065 rushing yards, becoming the first Jets back to top 1,000 since Chris Ivory in 2015.</p>
<h2 id="combine">The 2022 Combine Profile</h2>
<p>Hall left Iowa State as the consensus top back in his class, and the combine confirmed it. He ran an official 4.39-second 40-yard dash at 217 pounds, though his fastest run was originally clocked at 4.44 before the league adjusted the official time. The jumps were the headline: his 40-inch vertical was the best of any running back at that combine, and his 10-foot-6 broad jump tied for third. Next Gen Stats gave him an athleticism score of 99, one of only six running backs to earn that score since 2003.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Measurable</th><th>Breece Hall (2022)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Height / Weight</td><td>5'11", 217 lbs</td></tr>
<tr><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>4.39s (6th among RBs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical Jump</td><td>40" (best among RBs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broad Jump</td><td>10'6" (tied 3rd among RBs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Draft Slot</td><td>No. 36 overall, first RB selected</td></tr>
</table>
<p>That combination — a sub-4.4 forty at 217 pounds plus a 40-inch vertical — is the profile of a back who generates a huge amount of force in a short space. It is also why his game works in tight quarters: he can cut and accelerate without needing a long runway. Hall skipped the 3-cone and shuttle, so there is no official agility number, but the jumps and the 40 together describe elite lower-body power for his size.</p>
